
  Next question — this is the subsystem part of the escalation.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "track" can mean anything from a better log line to a real analytics pipeline, and the choice decides how
  much new structure this repo grows.

  - [1mLog line only[0m — [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m the id alongside the username, no new module. Buys you the id flowing through and
    visible in devtools; costs nothing; rules out nothing later. But nothing is actually recorded anywhere you can
    query.
  - [1mA [38;5;153mtrackLogin[39m function in app.js[0m — one small named function the handler calls with the login result. Buys you a
    single place to change when you do have a real destination, and it's honest about being a seam. Still no network
    call.
  - [1mA tracking module + POST[0m — a new [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m that posts events to an endpoint. Buys you real recorded events;
    costs a new module, a new endpoint to agree on, and error handling for when the tracking call itself fails.

  The first two are cheap to move between. The third is the one that commits you — a new module and a wire format that
  other code starts depending on.

  My recommendation is [1m[38;5;153mtrackLogin[39m function in app.js[0m. It gives "track who logged in" a real, named home without
  inventing an endpoint or a module for a codebase that currently has one stubbed API call and no tests. When you have a
  live backend, that function body is the only thing that changes.
